.TH CP 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
cp \- copy files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cp
.RB [ \-f ]
.I source_file target_file
.br
.B cp
.RB [ \-f ]
.I source_file
.RI [ source_file ]\ .\|.\|.
.I target_dir
.br
.B cp
.B \-R
.RB [ \-f ]
.I source_file
.RI [ source_file ]\ .\|.\|.
.I target
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cp
copies the contents of
.I source_file
to
.IR target_file .
If
.I source_file
is itself a symbolic link, its referent's contents are followed and
copied \(em the destination is always created as an ordinary file (or, with
.BR \-R ", a directory tree)."
.PP
If more than one
.I source_file
is given, or a single
.I source_file
is given together with a
.I target
that already exists as a directory, each
.I source_file
is copied into that directory under its own final pathname component
(that is, the destination is
.IR target_dir /basename( source_file )).
It is an error for more than one
.I source_file
to be given when the last operand does not name an existing directory.
.PP
With
.BR \-R ,
a
.I source_file
that names a directory is copied as a whole tree: the directory and
every entry beneath it are recreated under the destination. Symbolic
links, FIFOs, device nodes, and sockets found
.I inside
such a tree are not recreated; each one is reported with a diagnostic
and causes the overall exit status to be nonzero, but the rest of the
tree is still copied. Without
.BR \-R ,
a
.I source_file
that names a directory is skipped with a diagnostic rather than copied.
.PP
.B cp
refuses to copy a file onto itself (detected by matching device and
inode number between the resolved source and an existing destination),
and, with
.BR \-R ,
refuses to copy a directory into its own subtree. The self-subtree
check is a plain, case-insensitive textual comparison of the
.I source_file
and
.I target
operands as given, with
.B /
and
.B \e
treated as equivalent; it does not resolve symbolic links, "..", or
two differently-spelled paths that happen to name the same place, so
it catches the direct, common case rather than every disguised one.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-R
Copy directories recursively, as described above. The lowercase
.B \-r
is accepted as an exact synonym.
.TP
.B \-r
Synonym for
.BR \-R .
.TP
.B \-f
If a destination file cannot be opened for writing, remove it and
retry the open once. This applies only to the destination open
itself; it has no effect on any other failure (a read error, a write
error, or a failure encountered while walking a directory tree).
.TP
.B \-i
Not implemented.
.B cp
prints a diagnostic and exits immediately with status 2 rather than
prompting before an overwrite; there is no interactive-confirmation
behavior available for this option to enable.
.TP
.B \-p
Not implemented.
.B cp
prints a diagnostic and exits immediately with status 2 rather than
partially honor it: every file this build creates gets the platform's
ordinary default create permissions (governed by the umask), and every
directory it creates is made mode 0777 before the umask is applied,
regardless of the source's actual mode, owner, or timestamps.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-L ", " \-P
Not implemented.
These options select which symbolic links named directly on the
command line are followed; none of the three is implemented, so each
is refused with a diagnostic and exit status 2 rather than have
.B cp
silently pick one behavior to emulate.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I source_file
A pathname of a file or (with
.BR \-R )
directory to copy.
.TP
.I target_file
A pathname of the destination file, used only in the exactly-one-source
form.
.TP
.I target_dir
A pathname of an existing directory, used when copying one or more
source files into it under their own basenames.
.TP
.I target
A pathname of the destination, used with
.BR \-R ;
if it already exists as a directory, each
.I source_file
is copied into it as described above, otherwise it is used as the name
of a single copied tree.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand was copied successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred while copying one or more operands; the rest of the
operands were still attempted.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error occurred: an invalid or unsupported option, a missing
operand, a missing destination operand, or more than one
.I source_file
given with a
.I target
that is not an existing directory. No copying is attempted in this
case.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mv (1),
.BR rm (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR chmod (1)
